\section{Model}{

\deqn{dN/dt = R(t) - (\sigma_n + \tau) N,\quad
      dN_s/dt = \sigma_n N - \tau_s N_s,}
\deqn{dC/dt = \tau N - (\sigma_c + \alpha) C,\quad
      dC_s/dt = \tau_s N_s + \sigma_c C - \alpha_s C_s.}
\code{sigma_c} is a phenomenological post-export conversion rate. Simulating
with \code{sigma_c > 0} generates data from this kinetic model; it does not
represent or establish any molecular mechanism.
}

\section{Timing semantics}{

All times are on one experimental axis, in \code{time_unit}.
\itemize{
\item \code{onset_time} is the (gene-specific) transcriptional onset:
\code{R(t) = 0} before it. It may lie before \code{origin}; the history from
\code{onset_time} to \code{origin} is then integrated first.
\item \code{t_star} is the common intervention (shutoff) time. It is not
shifted by the onset: changing \code{onset_time} changes only the
transcriptional history before the intervention. No time shift is drawn
internally.
\item Transcription schedule (right-continuous): \code{R(t) = 0} for
\code{t < onset_time}; \code{R} for \verb{onset_time <= t < t_star}; and, from
\code{t_star} on, \code{0} (\code{"SHUTOFF"}), \code{residual_fraction * R}
(\code{"PSEUDO_SHUTOFF"}); \code{"NONE"} keeps \code{R} throughout.
\item \code{y0} is the state at \code{min(onset_time, origin)}. The trajectory is
integrated between consecutive points of the grid
\code{seq(origin, horizon, by = grid_step)} (plus the sampling times and
\code{t_star}) with \code{\link[deSolve:ode]{deSolve::ode()}} (method \code{lsoda}, default tolerances),
with onset and \code{t_star} as breakpoints. The manuscript benchmark used
\code{origin = 0}, \code{grid_step = 1}, \code{horizon = 1000} and \code{y0 = 0}. The
grid determines the integration intervals (and so values at the level
of the solver tolerance) but not the model.
}
}

\section{Replicates and noise}{

With \code{n_replicates > 1} and \code{param_cv > 0}, each replicate multiplies every
kinetic rate by an independent log-normal factor with coefficient of
variation \code{param_cv} (\code{R} is kept fixed), as in the frozen simulator; the
manuscript benchmark used \code{param_cv = 0.05} and 3, 5 or 10 replicates.
Replicates are independent destructive samples. \code{noise} adds assay noise
to the sampled states with the benchmark presets:
\code{platform} one of \code{"gaussian"}, \code{"rnaseq"}, \code{"rtqpcr"} and \code{level} one of
\code{"very_low"}, \code{"low"}, \code{"medium"}, \code{"high"} (benchmark labels GAUSS,
RNA-seq, RT-qPCR and Very low, Low, Medium, High). Gaussian noise can give
negative values (not truncated); RT-qPCR output is on the \code{2^-Ct} scale.
}

\section{Random numbers and reproducibility}{

Random numbers are drawn in this order: per replicate, one normal draw for
each of \verb{tau, tau_s, alpha, alpha_s, sigma_n, sigma_c, R} (the draw for \code{R}
is discarded, as in the frozen simulator); then the assay noise for the
states \verb{N, C, C_s, N_s}. No draws are made when \code{param_cv = 0} and
\code{noise = NULL}. With an explicit \code{seed}, \code{\link[=set.seed]{set.seed()}} is called first and
the caller's random-number state is restored afterwards; with
\code{seed = NULL} the global stream is used and consumed. ODE integration
(compiled \code{lsoda}) can differ across platforms at the level of
floating-point rounding; random draws are generated by R itself.
}

